## Guard Clauses Pattern

```xiom
fn transfer(from: &mut Account, to: &mut Account, amount: Int)
  requires: amount > 0;
{
  // Guard clause: validate all inputs at the top
  panic_if(from.id == to.id, "cannot transfer to the same account");
  panic_if(from.balance < amount, "insufficient funds");
  panic_if(from.is_frozen(), "source account is frozen");
  panic_if(to.is_frozen(), "destination account is frozen");

  // Main logic — all guards passed, state is valid
  from.balance = from.balance - amount;
  to.balance = to.balance + amount;
}
```

### Defense in Depth

```xiom
fn process_user_input(raw: Str) -> Result[Command, ValidationError] {
  // Layer 1: Length check
  if raw.len() == 0 { return Err(ValidationError::Empty); }
  if raw.len() > 1024 { return Err(ValidationError::TooLong); }

  // Layer 2: Character validation
  if raw.contains_null_byte() { return Err(ValidationError::InvalidChars); }

  // Layer 3: Parse and validate
  let cmd = parse_command(raw)?;

  // Layer 4: Business rules
  if !cmd.is_allowed() { return Err(ValidationError::Forbidden); }

  return Ok(cmd);
}
```

### Trust Boundaries

```xiom
// EXTERNAL boundary — validate everything
fn handle_http_request(req: HttpRequest) -> Response {
  // Validate ALL external input
  let user = validate_auth(req.headers)?;
  let body = validate_body(req.body)?;
  let params = validate_params(req.params)?;

  // INTERNAL call — trust the validated data
  return process_authenticated_request(user, body, params);
}
```

